---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/privy-vs-justuno';
const title = 'Privy vs Justuno for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Privy vs Justuno for Shopify — popup capture and onsite conversion vs gamification and A/B testing depth, email automation limits, and graduation paths.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Privy or Justuno better for a Shopify store doing $25k/mo?',
    answer: 'Revenue alone does not decide this choice. For an illustrative $25k monthly store, list the capture forms, welcome messages and cart recovery jobs you need. Evaluate Privy for the combined capture and email workflow and Justuno for onsite capture connected to your chosen sender. Verify each required feature and integration in the current plan; compare the staff time and full stack cost before choosing.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Privy vs Justuno pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Obtain dated quotes for the same traffic, contacts and sends rather than relying on an entry price. Include the capture plan, lifecycle sender, SMS usage and carrier charges where relevant, integrations, support and implementation time. Model ordinary months and peak traffic, annual commitments and overages. Neither the old starting prices nor the billing units on this page have been verified for publication.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better popup conversion rates?',
    answer: 'No measured conversion-rate winner is established here. Compare offers on randomly assigned eligible visitors, with stable creative and exposure rules. Measure unique signups, confirmed channel permission, downstream purchases and unsubscribes, alongside uncertainty. A larger signup count can reflect lower-quality acquisition; vendor-attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental sales.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Privy or Justuno replace Klaviyo for email?',
    answer: 'No for retention at scale. Both handle capture and basic automations — welcome, cart — adequately for early-stage stores. Neither matches Klaviyo browse depth, predictive segmentation, or complex suppression at 500+ orders/month. Plan graduation to Omnisend, Sequenzy, or Klaviyo when email becomes a line item, not a popup afterthought.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Privy or Justuno?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy fits post-capture lifecycle — welcome, cart, winback playbooks with pay-per-email pricing. Privy and Justuno win onsite conversion. Typical stack: Justuno or Privy for capture, Sequenzy or Omnisend for lifecycle. Sequenzy replaces the ESP layer Privy email outgrows around 300–500 monthly orders.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better A/B testing for popups?',
    answer: 'Verify which current plans support the experiment you need: creative, offer, trigger, audience allocation and reporting. Confirm whether simultaneous experiments collide and whether visitors retain their assigned variant. Use the same eligible audience and decide the outcome and sample requirements in advance. This comparison does not establish a current feature-tier winner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which integrates with Shopify more cleanly?',
    answer: 'Test the actual Shopify integration with your forms, storefront and selected sender. Check event delay, variant data, purpose-specific permission, unsubscribe propagation and recovery ownership. Inspect mobile layouts, repeated submissions and sync failures. App-store presence and familiarity do not establish reliable behavior in your stack.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Propose a controlled popup experiment, not a fixed two-week or 20% winning rule. Randomize eligible visitors, record exposure and consent, and follow cohorts through purchases and suppression. Predefine the observation window and report sample sizes and uncertainty. Separately test a purchase, unsubscribe, duplicate event and failed sync to confirm pending recovery is cancelled and replay does not create another send.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Privy vs Justuno</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Justuno for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          Two onsite conversion specialists merchants compare before graduating to full ESP platforms. Privy bundles popup capture with basic email automation — the default Shopify App Store answer for early-stage list growth. Justuno leads with gamification, A/B testing, and advanced onsite targeting for operators who treat popup optimization as a weekly discipline. The pottery, board game and outdoor examples below are proposed evaluation fixtures, not observed tests or merchant results. Current plan and integration claims require confirmation; order volume does not define a migration threshold.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Capture + basic email', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'One app for popups, welcome, and cart at early stage.' },
            { label: 'Gamification + A/B tests', tool: 'Justuno', reason: 'Spin wheels, scratch cards, and popup multivariate testing.' },
            { label: 'Shopify familiarity', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Wider agency adoption and simpler onboarding.' },
            { label: 'Signup rate optimization', tool: 'Justuno', reason: 'Built for operators who test popup creative weekly.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Lifecycle playbooks when capture tools outgrow their email layer.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Privy</th><th>Justuno</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Early capture + basic email</td><td>Gamified conversion optimization</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Dated capture + sender quote required</td><td>Dated onsite + integration quote required</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>Verify current billable units</td><td>Verify current billable units</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email automation</td><td>Basic — welcome, cart</td><td>Verify paired sender and workflow ownership</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A/B testing</td><td>Available on higher tiers</td><td>Core strength</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Gamification</td><td>Spin wheels available</td><td>Deep — scratch, countdown, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ESP graduation</td><td>When required workflows fail acceptance tests</td><td>Assess capture and sender separately</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Low</td><td>Low–moderate</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What this comparison is actually about</h2>
        <p>
          Separate onsite capture from newsletter and lifecycle delivery. A capture tool connected to an ESP does not itself own every downstream message. Privy is optimized when capture and basic campaigns are the bottleneck. Justuno is optimized when quiz and popup intent quality is the top-of-funnel problem. Verify the plan, connected sender and actual event path for each required workflow before comparing the total cost.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Camping Equipment Merchant ($78k/mo) — route quiz outcomes into different welcome branches before tent promos</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative camping equipment merchant fixture with 1,350 orders/mo at roughly $78k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows tent-capacity quizzes and family car-camping segments, which means email must handle trip-season browse recovery on mobile PDPs before cart before Memorial Day camping spike — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: route quiz outcomes into different welcome branches before tent promos.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the camping fixture, route tent-capacity answers into family and solo-trip education in the chosen sender. Keep the quiz purpose and channel permission separate. A family answer does not authorize SMS. Simulate a tent variant selling out between scheduling and dispatch; check current availability and cancel or replace the offer. Record which system supplies the quiz value, which owns the pending message, and how a changed answer updates the audience.
        </p>
        <h2>Before holiday starter-set rush: pottery studio retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative pottery studio fixture with 480 orders/mo at roughly $31k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets, which means email must handle kiln-care post-purchase and holiday gift exclusions before holiday starter-set rush — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: exclude class waitlist leads from glaze-kit promos after signup source tagging.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the pottery fixture, distinguish class waitlists from glaze-kit buyers. Route waitlist leads to class scheduling and safety information appropriate to their signup purpose; do not treat a class inquiry as permission for unrelated promotions. Test a sold-out kit and a recipient who buys a gift but does not use the kit. Verify suppression and inventory again at dispatch, with one owner cancelling stale queued offers.
        </p>
        <h2>Vinyl Record Shop: limited pressings with 200–400 unit runs</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative vinyl record shop fixture with 1,800 orders/mo at roughly $72k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows limited pressings with 200–400 unit runs, which means email must handle VIP drop allocation and grading education before promos before Record Store Day — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: suppress recent full-price buyers from instant discount blasts on scarce pressings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the vinyl fixture, separate pressing alerts, grading education and discount eligibility. A pressing-alert signup does not imply interest in every release. Simulate a 300-unit pressing becoming unavailable during a two-hour scheduled window; verify current variant availability before each offer. Test a full-price purchase after queue entry and ensure it removes the discount message. These are proposed checks, not evidence that either tool prevented complaints.
        </p>
        <h2>How outdoor gear retailer emails through Memorial Day sale week</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative outdoor gear retailer fixture with 2,400 orders/mo at roughly $112k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows layering guides and ultralight segment tags, which means email must handle Memorial Day tent promos only after education sends before Memorial Day sale week — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: merchandise ultralight versus family car-camping with different hero tents.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the outdoor fixture, separate ultralight and family car-camping interests and verify the hero tent matches the recorded segment. Test a gift purchase without inferring the recipient uses the equipment. Check current tent availability and suppression before dispatch. If email and SMS are both used, document each channel permission and queue owner, cancellation propagation and deduplication; a fixed delay does not make the sequence eligible.
        </p>
        <h2>Mechanical Keyboard Vendor ($58k/mo) — halt cart recovery when preorder tags flip to closed</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative mechanical keyboard vendor fixture with 940 orders/mo at roughly $58k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows six-week group-buy cycles on $180–$420 kits, which means email must handle GB close suppression and switch-type education across five PDPs before GB close deadlines — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: halt cart recovery when preorder tags flip to closed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the keyboard fixture, simulate a six-week group buy closing while recovery is queued. Verify the actual preorder state, switch type and kit availability before dispatch. A closed group buy must cancel stale purchase prompts in every sending queue. Replay the closure event after a failed sync and confirm it cannot reopen recovery or duplicate a send. Keep transactional preorder updates separate from promotional permission.
        </p>
        <h2>Before Essen release week: board game store retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          Use an illustrative board game store fixture with 1,050 orders/mo at roughly $67k/mo; these are scenario inputs, not merchant observations. Revenue rhythm follows pre-order allocation anxiety on hot titles, which means email must handle pre-order state machines and fulfillment pivots after allocation closes before Essen release week — not after support tickets spike. Privy contributes popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages; Justuno contributes advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: pause winback until fulfillment tags show shipped for pre-orders.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define peak-week priority explicitly: operational order updates, eligible recovery, welcome education and promotions need separate purposes and owners. A newsletter signup is not SMS permission or permission for every message type. Inspect current channel permission, suppression and purchase state immediately before dispatch. Name the system that owns each queue; a connected capture app must not create a second recovery sender. Test cancellation after purchase or opt-out, then replay the same event and confirm a stable event key prevents a duplicate send.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the board game fixture, simulate an allocation closing before Essen release week and a preorder changing from paid to shipped. Keep fulfillment updates separate from winback eligibility. Confirm the chosen sender receives the current allocation and fulfillment state, cancels obsolete offers, and does not replay a second recovery message after reconnecting. Track uncertainty in delivery timing rather than promising that a tag alone guarantees cancellation.
        </p>
        <h2>Tool friction where Privy and Justuno diverge on Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          Privy shines when capture and basic campaigns are the bottleneck. Its friction shows when replenishment timing and browse depth are core KPIs. Justuno shines when quiz and popup intent quality is the top-of-funnel problem. Its friction shows when you need a primary ESP without a paired sender. Merchants that ignore friction points buy the wrong ceiling — or the wrong floor.
        </p>
        <p>
          Model a full year with identical inputs: eligible contacts, visits, peak traffic, email volume and connected sender requirements. Include integration, implementation and operator time. A vinyl waitlist import and a seasonal outdoor broadcast are useful cost scenarios, not observed billing results. Confirm the current billable units and overages in dated quotes; do not infer a cheaper platform from a different denominator.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Newsletter layer: editorial merchandising without discount training</h2>
        <p>
          Newsletters still matter. Coffee origin stories, vinyl staff picks, keyboard switch explainers, and climbing trip reports build trust before product blocks. Identify the actual campaign sender in each stack. Verify how product data reaches it and how current inventory is checked when a limited run sells out; onsite targeting alone does not establish email delivery capability.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Pricing models that punish different mistakes</h2>
        <p>
          For an illustrative 12,000–18,000-contact list, quote the same eligible audience and annual traffic in both stacks. Record required capture, sender and integration charges, optional channel charges, support and migration time. Keep cold imports out of sending until their permission and current suppression are established. This contact range is a costing fixture, not a plan convergence or graduation threshold.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Hybrid stack patterns on Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          A proposed stack may combine onsite capture, a lifecycle sender and a separate SMS service. Verify each integration rather than assuming compatibility or legal compliance. Assign one recovery owner, purpose-specific permission checks and cross-system cancellation. Test purchase, opt-out, queue failure and event replay before enabling overlapping automation.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Privy if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Capture and basic campaigns are the bottleneck</li>
          <li>You will use popup capture and simple welcome or cart messages weekly</li>
          <li>The dated all-in quote fits your traffic and sending calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Privy without external rescue</li>
          <li>Switching cost favors Privy for the next twelve months</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Justuno if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Quiz and popup intent quality is the top-of-funnel problem</li>
          <li>You will use advanced popups, quizzes, and onsite capture weekly</li>
          <li>The dated capture-plus-sender quote fits your calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Justuno without external rescue</li>
          <li>A randomized test supports any incremental benefit claimed for the capture migration</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Privy is the better Shopify email tool when capture and basic campaigns are the bottleneck. Evaluate Justuno for onsite intent capture with an explicitly chosen lifecycle sender when that is the bottleneck. Run the required workflow tests in both stacks, model twelve-month billing with real sends and list hygiene, and compare attributed conversions with a randomized eligible holdout before claiming causal uplift, and pick a stack whose audience and cancellation checks pass before a drop — not whichever sales deck had the prettier logos. See <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models</a> before annual contracts.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">Sequenzy vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-privy">Klaviyo vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email tools for jewelry stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Justuno FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
